00:30:52.730 --> 00:30:53.090
<v Shirley Glass>Hello?
<v Shirley Glass>喂？

00:30:53.090 --> 00:30:54.090
<v Ira Glass>Hey, Mom.
<v Ira Glass>嘿，妈。

00:30:54.090 --> 00:30:54.820
<v Shirley Glass>Oh, hi, Ira.
<v Shirley Glass>哦，嗨，Ira。

00:30:54.820 --> 00:30:55.560
<v Ira Glass>How you doing?
<v Ira Glass>你好吗？

00:30:55.560 --> 00:30:56.770
<v Shirley Glass>Fine. Can you hold on a second?
<v Shirley Glass>挺好的。你能稍等一下吗？

00:30:56.770 --> 00:31:01.320
<v Ira Glass>Sure.
<v Ira Glass>当然。

00:31:01.320 --> 00:31:18.400
<v Ira Glass>This is what it's like with my parents. They're so busy. Call them. Put on hold. [SINGING] Baby, what's your hurry?
<v Ira Glass>这就是我父母平时的样子。他们太忙了。打给他们。让你挂机等待。[唱歌] 宝贝，你着急什么？

00:31:18.400 --> 00:31:28.460
<v Ira Glass>When I call my little sister, she works at Disney, and so there's Disney music playing on the hold system. But there's a lot of Disney music. And there's a lot of it that peo--
<v Ira Glass>当我给我的小妹妹打电话时，她在迪士尼工作，所以候线时会播放迪士尼音乐。但这迪士尼音乐太多了。而且有很多人……

00:31:28.460 --> 00:31:28.750
<v Shirley Glass>Hi.
<v Shirley Glass>喂。

00:31:28.750 --> 00:31:29.820
<v Ira Glass>Hi, Mom.
<v Ira Glass>嗨，妈。

00:31:29.820 --> 00:31:31.000
<v Shirley Glass>Yeah.
<v Shirley Glass>嗯。

00:31:31.000 --> 00:31:31.620
<v Ira Glass>It's me.
<v Ira Glass>是我。

00:31:31.620 --> 00:31:33.070
<v Shirley Glass>Yeah.
<v Shirley Glass>嗯。

00:31:33.070 --> 00:31:38.160
<v Ira Glass>Listen, can I record a quick conversation with you about something?
<v Ira Glass>听着，我能和你录一段简短的对话吗？

00:31:38.160 --> 00:31:39.120
<v Shirley Glass>What about?
<v Shirley Glass>关于什么的？

00:31:39.120 --> 00:31:53.840
<v Ira Glass>Well, you know the new show goes on the air this week. And as part of the show, we were thinking about having me call around to different people and get advice from them. And I wanted to know if you would have any advice.
<v Ira Glass>嗯，你知道新节目这周开播。作为节目的一部分，我们想让我给不同的人打电话征求他们的建议。我想知道你有什么建议吗？

00:31:53.840 --> 00:32:01.490
<v Shirley Glass>Hm. Do I have any advice? Well, can I ask another question?
<v Shirley Glass>嗯。我有建议吗？好吧，我可以问个问题吗？

00:32:01.490 --> 00:32:02.150
<v Ira Glass>Sure.
<v Ira Glass>当然。

00:32:02.150 --> 00:32:04.580
<v Shirley Glass>Who is your target audience?
Shirley Glass: 你的目标听众是谁？

00:32:04.580 --> 00:32:08.720
<v Ira Glass>You are such a pro.
Ira Glass: 你真是个行家。

00:32:08.720 --> 00:32:21.000
<v Shirley Glass>I'm saying that you're in danger of appealing to a narrow range of listeners if it becomes a little too-- I don't know what word to use.
Shirley Glass: 我是说，如果受众面变得太——我不知道该用什么词——你可能会面临听众群体过窄的风险。

00:32:21.000 --> 00:32:21.770
<v Ira Glass>Artsy.
Ira Glass: 艺术化？

00:32:21.770 --> 00:32:24.480
<v Shirley Glass>Artsy, yeah.
Shirley Glass: 对，艺术化。

00:32:24.480 --> 00:32:45.220
<v Ira Glass>Are you and Dad still worried about me making a living in public radio? I know just for years, you were urging me to just get out and get, basically, any job in TV that I possibly could. But now that I've got my own show, are you guys still worried? Or do you feel like things are going OK? Do you want me to get into television still?
Ira Glass: 你和爸爸还在担心我能不能靠公共广播过活吗？我知道过去好多年，你们一直催我辞职，去电视行业找份工作。但现在我有自己的节目了，你们还担心吗？还是觉得一切都还不错？你们还是想让我进电视圈吗？

00:32:45.220 --> 00:32:57.390
<v Shirley Glass>Now that Hugh Grant is such a big star, and everybody who sees you or sees your picture thinks how much you look like Hugh Grant, that fires up that TV thing again in me.
Shirley Glass: 既然休·格兰特现在是大明星了，而每个见过你或看过你照片的人都觉得你长得特别像他，这就又让我燃起了让你去干电视的念头。

00:32:57.390 --> 00:33:19.140
<v Ira Glass>All right. I'm stopping the tape. This is me live. That was the tape. Only my mother could possibly believe this. Only a mother could pretty much believe this. Other adults see me, and the thought that goes through their head is not Hugh Grant. The thought that goes through their head is tall Jew.
Ira Glass: 好了，我暂停录音了。现在是我在实时说话。刚才那是录音。只有我妈才会相信这种话。真的，只有妈妈才会相信。别的成年人见到我，脑子里闪过的念头可不是休·格兰特，而是“一个高个子犹太人”。

00:33:19.140 --> 00:33:31.310
<v Shirley Glass>Well, gosh, wouldn't they want this wonderful humanistic, intelligent reporter who looks like Hugh Grant?
Shirley Glass: 哎呀，难道他们就不想拥有这么一位既有人文关怀、聪明睿智，又长得像休·格兰特的记者吗？

00:33:31.310 --> 00:33:32.300
<v Ira Glass>All right. Yeah, let's move on.
Ira Glass: 行吧，咱们继续。

00:33:32.300 --> 00:33:33.560
<v Shirley Glass>What's the theme for this week?
Shirley Glass: 这周的主题是什么？

00:33:33.560 --> 00:33:45.040
<v Ira Glass>The theme for this week is new beginnings. And we have several stories, people telling about various ways in which their life began anew at some point.
Ira Glass: 本周的主题是“新的开始”。我们有几个故事，讲述了人们如何在人生的某个节点迎来各种形式的新生。

00:33:45.040 --> 00:33:50.500
<v Shirley Glass>That's very interesting, because I just did an interview this morning with a newspaper reporter about roman--
Shirley Glass: 这太有趣了，因为今天早上我刚接受了一位报社记者的采访，谈的是浪漫……

00:33:50.500 --> 00:34:08.260
<v Ira Glass>I'm just going to stop the tape again. This is my whole life. I call my mom for an interview, and it's not even her first interview of the day. I was lucky to get a booking. She's a therapist, and sometimes she gets called by the papers and stuff.
Ira Glass: 我又要暂停录音了。这就是我的生活常态。我给妈妈打电话想采访她，结果这竟然还不是她今天的第一场采访。我能预约到她都算运气好。她是心理治疗师，报纸之类的媒体经常找她。

00:34:08.260 --> 00:34:21.630
<v Shirley Glass>--mance, romantic love. And people's expectations about relationships. And one of the things I believe is that there are a lot of people who are good at beginnings, but they're not good at middles.
Shirley Glass: ……浪漫之爱。以及人们对关系的期望。我的观点之一是，很多人擅长开始，但不擅长经营过程。

00:34:21.630 --> 00:34:22.350
<v Ira Glass>Which means what?
Ira Glass: 这话怎么讲？

00:34:22.350 --> 00:34:34.720
<v Shirley Glass>It means that they like the beginning, where there's all this idealization and romantic projections. And the other person can be who they think they should be, rather than who they are. And when they get to the middle phase--
Shirley Glass: 这意味着他们喜欢开始阶段，那时候充满了理想化和浪漫投射。对方可以成为他们心目中认为的样子，而不是真实的自我。当进入中期阶段时……

00:34:34.720 --> 00:34:55.720
<v Ira Glass>All right. I'm just going to stop the tape. Listen, all of you in the audience right now, let's just agree right now, it's the very beginning of our relationship. It's the very beginning of our radio relationship right now. This is our little first little radio date, and I just don't want any idealizing on either side. OK? Let's just make eye contact right now. Remember what Joe Franklin said about the eye contact. No idealizing.
Ira Glass: 好了，我要再按一下暂停。听着，现在在听节目的各位，让我们达成共识，现在正是我们这段关系的开端。这是我们广播缘分的起点，是我们第一次广播约会，我可不希望任何一方过度理想化。好吗？我们现在注视着对方。记得乔·富兰克林说过的关于眼神交流的事吗？不要理想化。

00:34:55.720 --> 00:35:04.110
<v Shirley Glass>--where there's more of a reality-based relationship, they run away from it because it's not as exciting.
Shirley Glass: ……也就是当关系回归现实时，他们就会逃之夭夭，因为觉得没那么刺激了。

00:35:04.110 --> 00:35:32.860
<v Ira Glass>It's interesting that you say that because, actually, as we've approached the first show, I've realized that I am much more comfortable with the notion of every day, work-a-day radio work, and being on every week, and having pieces on the air. But the notion of saying in a really big way, "OK, this is the beginning. It's the beginning, and we're going to have a big beginning, and we're going to make an epic statement," I feel very uncomfortable with.
Ira Glass: 你这么说很有趣，因为实际上，随着第一期节目的临近，我意识到我对那种平平淡淡、日复一日的广播工作，以及每周按时播送节目的状态感到自在得多。但如果让我郑重其事地宣布：“好，这是开始。这是一个盛大的开始，我们要做出史诗般的宣言”，我反而会感到非常不适。

00:35:32.860 --> 00:35:33.640
<v Shirley Glass>So you are good at middles.
Shirley Glass: 所以你擅长经营过程。

00:35:33.640 --> 00:35:36.650
<v Ira Glass>I'm better, I think, at middles than at beginnings.
Ira Glass: 我想，我确实比应对开始，更擅长经营过程。

00:35:36.650 --> 00:35:41.810
<v Shirley Glass>That's good. That's good because practically all of life is the middle.
Shirley Glass: 那很好，那很好，因为人生的绝大部分时间都是过程。

00:35:41.810 --> 00:36:05.550
<v Ira Glass>We have gotten so deep here. I never expected that it was going to get so deep. I'm just very pleased at how deep this has gotten. Now you're sitting there, you're thinking, "Is he making fun of me? What's happening now? Where are you?" No, I'm not. I'm not, actually. I'm not. I'm not. I'm not. You have nothing to worry about.
Ira Glass: 我们聊得太深了。我从没想过会聊到这个深度。我很高兴能聊得这么深入。现在你坐在那儿肯定在想：“他在取笑我吗？怎么回事？你在哪儿？”不，我没有。真的没有。你不用担心。

00:36:05.550 --> 00:36:10.160
<v Shirley Glass>Are we going to get a tape of this since we're outside the Chicago listening area?
Shirley Glass: 我们能拿到这期的录音带吗？毕竟我们在芝加哥收听范围之外。

00:36:10.160 --> 00:36:12.150
<v Ira Glass>Depending on how you sound, yeah.
Ira Glass: 得看你表现得怎么样吧，可以。

00:36:12.150 --> 00:36:14.260
<v Shirley Glass>OK.
Shirley Glass: 好吧。

00:36:14.260 --> 00:36:25.910
<v Ira Glass>Well, that's my mom, Shirley Glass, speaking to us from Baltimore. I don't think she's going to get a tape. I do not think she's going to get a tape.
Ira Glass: 刚才那是我的母亲 Shirley Glass 在巴尔的摩接受采访。我想她拿不到录音带了。我觉得她拿不到。

00:36:25.910 --> 00:36:42.550
<v Ira Glass>Well, next on our little playhouse stage, we have Mr. Lawrence Steger. Now those of you who have been listening carefully to our program and taking notes, and I know there are many of you out there, you know what I'm supposed to do at this point. You know because you were listening carefully to Joe Franklin at the very beginning of our program.
Ira Glass: 好了，接下来在我们的剧场舞台上，我们要请出 Lawrence Steger 先生。各位认真收听我们节目并做笔记的听众——我知道你们大有人在——你们知道我这时候该做什么了。你们知道，因为你们刚才仔细听了 Joe Franklin 在节目开头的叮嘱。

00:36:42.550 --> 00:36:44.400
<v Joe Franklin>Above all, get the plug fast.
Joe Franklin: 最重要的是，赶紧打广告。

00:36:44.400 --> 00:37:06.540
<v Ira Glass>That's right. Well, thank you, Joe Franklin. Lawrence Steger is a filmmaker and a performance artist, just back from performing in Glasgow, Scotland. He's going back there in January to collaborate with the performance artist, Ron Athey, who lives here in Chicago. And basically, we contacted Lawrence, and we told him the story that you heard over the course of the last half-hour, Kevin Kelly's story, this guy who believes he only has six months to live, goes on this cross-country road trip.
Ira Glass: 没错，谢谢你，Joe Franklin。Lawrence Steger 是一位电影制作人和行为艺术家，刚从苏格兰格拉斯哥的演出回来。一月份他还要回去，与住在芝加哥的行为艺术家 Ron Athey 合作。我们联系了 Lawrence，告诉他你们在过去半小时里听到的故事，也就是 Kevin Kelly 的故事，这个被告知只剩六个月寿命的人，决定踏上横跨全国的公路旅行。

00:37:06.540 --> 00:37:32.610
<v Ira Glass>And as it turned out, when Lawrence Steger found out, the day that he found out that he was HIV positive, this was five years ago, on that day, it was the exact day that he and a friend left on a cross-country car trip. So we commissioned him to do a little piece about his experience. And here it is.
Ira Glass: 巧合的是，Lawrence Steger 五年前得知自己 HIV 呈阳性的那一天，正是他和朋友出发进行横跨全国公路旅行的日子。于是我们委托他创作了一段关于那段经历的小短片。下面就请听。

00:37:32.610 --> 00:38:03.880
<v Lawrence Steger>Title. Road. Treatment. It's shot entirely on video, mostly handheld. Shaky, out of focus, bad color. Overblown color actually. Sort of the way colors are separated on an old television console, yet still has all the outlines of the images repeated. The outlines of the images, the silhouettes, repeated over and over, ad nauseam, and fading into each other.
Lawrence Steger: 标题：路。治疗。视频全是用手持摄像机拍的。画面晃动，对焦不准，色彩糟糕。其实是色彩过曝了。就像老式电视机里的颜色分离，图像的轮廓反复重叠，一直叠到让人厌烦，最后融为一体。

00:38:03.880 --> 00:38:07.200
<v Lawrence Steger>Can I get this microphone adjusted a little, so I don't have to lean over so much?
Lawrence Steger: 能帮我调一下麦克风吗？这样我就不用弯着腰了。

00:38:07.200 --> 00:38:09.930
<v Ira Glass>Yeah, sure. Just pull that.
Ira Glass: 当然，拉一下那个就行。

00:38:09.930 --> 00:38:18.110
<v Lawrence Steger>Check one, two, three. Sound better? Yeah. Sorry. Thanks.
Lawrence Steger: 喂，一、二、三。声音好点了吗？好的。抱歉，谢了。

00:38:18.110 --> 00:38:41.100
<v Lawrence Steger>Synopsis. The story concerns Luke, gay, white, Midwestern, late twenties. Follows Luke on the day that he is informed of his HIV positive status. Luke cops a stance of cold, brittle, not unlike the Harrison Ford narration on Blade Runner, but there's a hint of vulnerability to Luke.
Lawrence Steger: 剧情大纲。故事关于 Luke，男，同性恋，白人，来自中西部，二十多岁。讲述 Luke 在得知自己 HIV 阳性当天发生的事。Luke 摆出一副冷漠、僵硬的态度，有点像《银翼杀手》里哈里森·福特的旁白，但 Luke 的内心深处还有一丝脆弱。

00:38:41.100 --> 00:38:43.730
<v Lawrence Steger>Have we got the Harrison Ford or the Rutger Hauer voice?
Lawrence Steger: 我们用的是哈里森·福特的声音还是鲁特格尔·哈尔的声音？

00:38:43.730 --> 00:38:44.670
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
Ira Glass: 对。

00:38:44.670 --> 00:38:47.660
<v Lawrence Steger>Great. Roll it.
Lawrence Steger: 好极了，播放。

00:38:47.660 --> 00:38:54.920
<v Harrison Ford>Enhance 5719. Track 45 left.
Harrison Ford: 增强 5719。追踪左侧 45 度。

00:38:54.920 --> 00:39:09.980
<v Lawrence Steger>Great. Take it under me. That's great. Story follows Luke. He's accompanied by his college buddy, Bill, and both are packed for a road trip across the country to San Francisco.
Lawrence Steger: 太棒了，把背景音乐调小。就这样。故事跟随 Luke。他由大学好友 Bill 陪同，两人打好行囊，准备开车横跨全国前往旧金山。

00:39:09.980 --> 00:39:14.790
<v Lawrence Steger>This isn't the right section of Blade Runner. Can you just kill the Blade Runner?
Lawrence Steger: 这不是《银翼杀手》合适的那一段。能把《银翼杀手》的声音关了吗？

00:39:14.790 --> 00:39:36.820
<v Lawrence Steger>Locations. Car interior. Gas station exterior. HIV clinic parking lot. HIV clinic interior. Highway. Music. Strauss's four last songs, particularly "Ruhe, meine Seele!" sung by Dame Janet Baker.
Lawrence Steger: 地点。车内。加油站外。艾滋病诊所停车场。诊所内部。高速公路。音乐。施特劳斯的《最后四首歌》，特别是 Dame Janet Baker 演唱的《休息吧，我的灵魂！》。

00:39:36.820 --> 00:39:42.010
<v Lawrence Steger>Can you take it under me? Hold.
Lawrence Steger: 能把音量调小吗？停一下。

00:39:42.010 --> 00:39:51.140
<v Lawrence Steger>Follows Luke and his friend Bill to the gas station and to the clinic, the last stop before getting on the highway.
Lawrence Steger: 讲述 Luke 和朋友 Bill 前往加油站和诊所的过程，那是上高速公路前的最后一站。

00:39:51.140 --> 00:39:55.250
<v Lawrence Steger>OK. Take it out. Take out the Janet Baker.
Lawrence Steger: 好的。切掉。把 Janet Baker 的歌切掉。

00:39:55.250 --> 00:40:02.260
<v Lawrence Steger>Bill loads one hits of pot while driving on the way to the gas station and to the clinic.
Lawrence Steger: Bill 在去往加油站和诊所的路上边开车边抽大麻。

00:40:02.260 --> 00:40:19.060
<v Lawrence Steger>Can we nix that Strauss music? It's too mournful. There's really no music on the soundtrack. It's stark, crisp. Maybe some songs coming from the radio at the clinic's desk?
Lawrence Steger: 能把那段施特劳斯的音乐撤了吗？太哀伤了。原声带里其实根本没音乐。应该是清冷、干脆的感觉。也许诊所前台的收音机里传出几首歌？

00:40:19.060 --> 00:40:19.880
<v Lawrence Steger>[RADIO STATIC]
Lawrence Steger: [收音机静电噪声]

00:40:19.880 --> 00:40:30.560
<v Ira Glass>Great. And definitely from the car radio mixed with surfing on an AM radio. No music.
<v Ira Glass>太棒了。这绝对是车里的收音机混合着AM电台搜台的声音。没有音乐。

00:40:30.560 --> 00:41:05.400
<v Ira Glass>The drama is constantly being undermined through the cool, collective quality of Luke's demeanor. He seems detached, quote, "I am not sure how I feel. I feel a little sad, sort of mad. I guess blank. But I'm OK. I'm sure I'll get a handle on it," end quote. Luke thinks he's sounding like a short story assignment in a creative writing class or, worse, trapped inside an artsy novel.
<v Ira Glass>卢克（Luke）那冷静、沉稳的举止不断地消解着剧情的张力。他显得很超脱，引用他的话说：“我不确定自己是什么感觉。我感到有点难过，也有点生气。我想是一片空白吧。但我还行。我相信我会处理好的。”卢克自认为听起来像创意写作课上的短篇小说作业，或者更糟的是，觉得自己被困在了一部矫揉造作的小说里。

00:41:05.400 --> 00:41:26.040
<v Ira Glass>Luke imagines himself in a television dramatization of himself. Camera pulls back from behind Luke's head, sort of on a mini-crane. Camera floats, hovers over the back of Luke's head. "The ceiling of the car must be incredibly high," he thinks to himself.
<v Ira Glass>卢克想象自己置身于一部关于他自己的电视改编剧中。摄像机从卢克脑后拉开，像是在一个小摇臂上。镜头浮动着，盘旋在卢克脑后。他心里想：“车顶一定高得离谱。”

00:41:26.040 --> 00:41:58.120
<v Ira Glass>Bill pulls into the closest parking spot in the clinic, blows out the last of the one-hit, and, as he's knocking the brass pipe into the ashtray, turns to Luke with that slightly watery look in his eyes from too much intake. Luke takes it as one of those Care Bear looks that he's experienced before from Bill. A little clumsy since Bill has to force his face into a sympathetic posture. Quick close-up on the corner of Bill's mouth.
<v Ira Glass>比尔（Bill）把车停在诊所最近的停车位上，呼出最后一口烟，当他把铜烟斗在烟灰缸里磕掉时，转过头看向卢克。因为吸多了，他的眼睛看起来有点湿润。卢克把它看作是比尔之前对他那种“爱心熊”式的关怀。这动作有点笨拙，因为比尔不得不强迫自己的脸摆出一副同情的表情。镜头快速特写比尔的嘴角。

00:41:58.120 --> 00:42:25.370
<v Ira Glass>There's a moment of anger flashing in Luke when he registers Bill's look. When Bill asks him, "What are you thinking about?" Luke responds, "Who's thinking? Nothing." "I hate thinking I'm in a novel," he thinks to himself. Cut to interior of the clinic, the reception area.
<v Ira Glass>当卢克注意到比尔的表情时，心中闪过一丝愤怒。当比尔问他：“你在想什么？”卢克回答说：“谁在想？什么也没想。”他心里暗想：“我讨厌那种觉得自己活在小说里的感觉。”切到诊所内部，接待处。

00:42:25.370 --> 00:42:29.320
<v Ira Glass>Can we change this sound bed here?
<v Ira Glass>我们能换一下这里的背景音乐吗？

00:42:29.320 --> 00:42:32.270
<v Ira Glass>[ROMANTIC MUSIC PLAYING]
<v Ira Glass>[浪漫的音乐响起]

00:42:32.270 --> 00:42:40.520
<v Ira Glass>Great. Take it down just a-- Great.
<v Ira Glass>太棒了。稍微调低一点——很好。

00:42:40.520 --> 00:43:34.490
<v Ira Glass>In the scene that we talked about on the phone, the clinic waiting room scene, that scene flips back and forth between various security black-and-white cameras mounted at the ceilings. The nurse assigned to Luke's anonymous number is a black drag queen named Stephanie, who wears a full nurse's outfit complete with a little paper hat that sits atop of her freshly coiffed hairdo. She's the only one in the clinic who wears a real uniform. Stephanie has the longest fingernails that Luke has ever seen on anyone. Luke thinks briefly about how the fingernails keep on growing even after a person dies, but he pushes that thought away with this fingers to his forehead, wonders why he is thinking about that. It's that novel thing again.
<v Ira Glass>在我们电话里谈过的那场戏，就是诊所候诊室的那场戏，镜头在天花板上安装的各种黑白监控摄像头之间来回切换。分配给卢克匿名编号的护士是一位名叫斯蒂芬妮（Stephanie）的黑人变装皇后，她穿着全套护士服，发型精心打理过，上面还顶着一顶小纸帽。她是诊所里唯一穿正式制服的人。斯蒂芬妮留着卢克见过的人中最长的指甲。卢克简短地想到指甲在人死后还会继续生长，但他用手指抵住前额，把这个念头推开了，奇怪自己为什么会想这个。又是那种小说里的东西在作祟。

00:43:34.490 --> 00:44:03.680
<v Ira Glass>Stephanie, the drag queen nurse, walks Luke back to the small cubicles that the tests are administered in and then used to relay the results. Luke's narrator imagines how many people have been in these cubicles and what they would look like if they were all piled on top of one another. Piles of tested bodies. Cut to Stephanie closing the hollow core door, makes that hollow core door sound.
<v Ira Glass>变装皇后护士斯蒂芬妮领着卢克回到进行测试的小隔间，那里也用于传达结果。卢克的叙述者想象着有多少人曾经待过这些隔间，如果把他们全都堆在一起会是什么样子。堆积如山的被测试者尸体。切到斯蒂芬妮关上那扇空心门，发出那种空心门关上的声音。

00:44:03.680 --> 00:44:05.720
<v Ira Glass>Do we have that on cart?
<v Ira Glass>我们有那段音效吗？

00:44:05.720 --> 00:44:07.530
<v Ira Glass>[SOUND OF HOLLOW DOOR CLOSING]
<v Ira Glass>[空心门关上的声音]

00:44:07.530 --> 00:44:34.230
<v Ira Glass>Perfect. Maybe a shot from a security camera that shows all of the cubicles in the clinic. Luke imagines himself in a George Tooker painting that was reproduced in his sixth grade reader. He wonders what his sixth grade teacher would think of Stephanie. He wonders if his sixth grade teacher was ever tested. He imagines her body in the pileup of bodies who have come to the clinic.
<v Ira Glass>完美。或许可以来一个监控摄像头的镜头，拍到诊所里所有的隔间。卢克想象自己置身于一幅乔治·图克（George Tooker）的画中，那是他六年级课本里印刷过的作品。他想知道他的六年级老师会对斯蒂芬妮有什么看法。他好奇他的六年级老师是否也被检测过。他想象着她的身体也在那些来到诊所的人堆里。

00:44:34.230 --> 00:45:03.590
<v Ira Glass>Stephanie has been saying something, and Luke has to blink his eyes again to refocus. He explains to Stephanie that he has been expecting this result, that he's experienced a large share of AIDS, cared for, and, likewise, buried lots of his friends. But it doesn't seem to come as a surprise. Stephanie says, "You can cry or hold my hand. I just want you to sit for a moment and let it sink in." Luke thinks, "Whatever."
<v Ira Glass>斯蒂芬妮一直在说着什么，卢克不得不再次眨眼让自己回过神来。他向斯蒂芬妮解释说他早就预料到了这个结果，他经历过很多艾滋病病例，照顾过，也埋葬过很多朋友。但这似乎并没有让他感到意外。斯蒂芬妮说：“你可以哭出来，或者握住我的手。我只是想让你坐一会儿，让情绪沉淀一下。”卢克心想：“随便吧。”

00:45:03.590 --> 00:45:39.070
<v Ira Glass>Cut to Bill in waiting room, flipping through People 's The Year in Pictures. Cut back to close-up of Luke, forehead wrinkled. He thinks his narrator wants him to get out of the cubicle. He waits for Stephanie to finish her spiel, thanks her, and shakes her hand, getting a slight scrape from one of the fingernails. Close-up on Luke's hand, no scratch. The walls seem to pulsate as Luke walks down the hallway to the reception area. He tries to be as blank as possible to Bill.
<v Ira Glass>切到坐在候诊室里的比尔，翻看着《人物》杂志的《年度图片》。切回卢克的特写，眉头紧锁。他觉得他的叙述者想让他离开隔间。他等着斯蒂芬妮说完那些客套话，感谢了她，并与她握手，指甲在他手上划了一下。特写卢克的手，没有抓痕。当卢克走回接待处时，墙壁似乎在脉动。他努力在比尔面前表现得尽可能空白。

00:45:39.070 --> 00:45:55.070
<v Ira Glass>I'm not sure about this final section. I know that we talked about it ending on the highway with the car being surrounded by bikers on their way to the Sturgis bikers' rally. But now I like the idea of it ending on the highway entrance ramp.
<v Ira Glass>我不确定这最后一段。我知道我们讨论过以高速公路为结尾，车子被一群前往斯特吉斯摩托车集会的骑手包围。但现在我喜欢以高速公路入口匝道结尾的创意。

00:45:55.070 --> 00:46:27.250
<v Ira Glass>Cut to interior of car pulling out of parking lot. Luke keeps looking straight ahead, as he murmurs, "I'm positive." Long, slow pan from the back of Luke's head to the back of Bill's. There's no reaction in either of their faces or, better, the profiles of their faces. This is the longest shot. They don't look at each other.
<v Ira Glass>切到车内，车辆驶离停车场。卢克一直直视前方，喃喃自语：“我是阳性。”一个长长的、缓慢的推镜头，从卢克的后脑勺移到比尔的后脑勺。他们的脸上都没有什么反应，或者说，侧脸的轮廓上没有反应。这是最长的一个镜头。他们没有看对方。

00:46:27.250 --> 00:46:58.840
<v Ira Glass>Perhaps this scene would be shot in blue screen with the camera in the backseat and the sky surrounding the two heads of Bill and Luke having that old, scratchy, 16 millimeter time lapse exposure, so the clouds seem to be moving at a rapid pace. Flickers, flips back and forth between real sky and blue screen backdrop. Voice comes up on a car radio. "Trying not to think of the future. Just live in the present moment." Something like that. You got that? It comes onto the radio.
<v Ira Glass>也许这场戏可以用蓝幕拍摄，摄像机放在后座，比尔和卢克的两个头周围的天空有着那种老式、带划痕的16毫米延时曝光效果，云层看起来在快速移动。画面在真实天空和蓝幕背景之间闪烁跳动。汽车收音机里传出声音。“尽量不要去想未来。活在当下。”类似这样的话。你听到了吗？声音从收音机里传出来。

00:46:58.840 --> 00:47:19.680
<v Kevin Kelly>I also decided that it was an entirely unnatural and inhumane way to live, and that having a future is part of what being human is about, and that when you take away the future for humans, you take away a lot of the humanness, and that it's not actually a very good thing to live entirely in the present, that one needs to have a past.
<v Kevin Kelly>我还认定那是一种极其不自然且不人道的生活方式，拥有未来是身为人类的一部分。当你剥夺了人类的未来，你就剥夺了很大一部分人性。事实上，完全活在当下并不是一件好事，人需要有过去。

00:47:19.680 --> 00:48:24.230
<v Lawrence Steger>Luke comments to Bill, "Live entirely in the present, huh?" Bill drives and looks out the corner of his right eye to see what position Luke is holding his head in. Luke looks outside passenger window and, every once in a while, turns to glance at Bill. Long pause. There's dead air. Cut to Luke's point of view. Car is pulling onto entrance ramp of highway. Luke sees hitchhiker with a sign that he stands for any remote meaning to the narrative. Luke sees himself outside of his own story. He can't read the hitchhiker's sign. He knows that he's on a long silent journey. He leans over to turn off the radio. Cut to black.
<v Lawrence Steger>卢克对一旁的比尔说：“活在当下，哈？”比尔开着车，用右眼余光瞥了一眼卢克正以什么姿势低着头。卢克看着副驾窗外，偶尔转头看一眼比尔。长久的停顿。出现了死寂。切到卢克的视角。车子正开上高速公路的入口匝道。卢克看到一个举着牌子的搭车人，他代表着叙事中任何遥远的意义。卢克看到了故事之外的自己。他读不懂搭车人的牌子。他知道自己正处于一段漫长的沉默旅程中。他俯身关掉了收音机。画面切黑。

00:48:24.230 --> 00:49:39.230
<v Lawrence Steger>[MUSIC - "(UH-OH) GET OUT OF THE CAR" BY THE TRENIERS]
<v Lawrence Steger>[音乐 - The Treniers 演唱的 “(Uh-Oh) Get Out of the Car”]

00:49:39.230 --> 00:49:45.650
<v Ira Glass>Well, Lawrence Steger is a Chicago performance artist and filmmaker.
<v Ira Glass>嗯，劳伦斯·斯蒂格（Lawrence Steger）是一位芝加哥的行为艺术家和电影制作人。

00:49:45.650 --> 00:49:55.320
<v Ira Glass>This is Your Radio Playhouse. I'm Ira Glass. OK, what am I doing right now? What am I doing right now? That's right. That's right. Eye contact.
<v Ira Glass>这里是《广播剧场》（Your Radio Playhouse）。我是艾拉·格拉斯（Ira Glass）。好吧，我现在在做什么？我现在在做什么？没错。没错。眼神交流。

00:49:55.320 --> 00:49:57.660
<v Joe Franklin>Look right in the eye. Eye contact is everything.
<v Joe Franklin>直视眼睛。眼神交流就是一切。

00:49:57.660 --> 00:50:14.760
<v Ira Glass>That's right. Advice from the master. OK. Let's review our program so far. Let's just review. Let's just get things straight right now. Our stories so far have been about people whose futures were taken from them and were thrown into the present in one way or another.
<v Ira Glass>说得对。来自大师的建议。好的。让我们回顾一下我们的节目。让我们理一理。把事情搞清楚。到目前为止，我们的故事讲述的都是那些未来被剥夺，以各种方式被抛入当下的人们。

00:50:14.760 --> 00:50:44.830
<v Ira Glass>I guess when any big emotional moment happens, you are thrown in the present in a really aggressive, aggressive way, whether you choose to or not. You have this hyper sense of reality. Well, the next story is about someone whose future was taken from him when he was wrongly imprisoned for 20 years. And his sentence was commuted two years ago. And he found himself reborn into everyday life.
<v Ira Glass>我想每当重大情感时刻发生时，你都会以一种非常剧烈的方式被抛入当下，无论你是否选择这么做。你会有一种对现实的超强感知。那么，下一个故事讲述的是一个未来被剥夺的人，他被错误监禁了20年。他的刑期在两年前减免了。他发现自己重生到了日常生活中。

00:50:44.830 --> 00:50:55.550
<v Ed Ryder>It was like I just-- I don't know. It seemed like the oxygen was even different. You know what I mean? The air seemed to be thinner.
<v Ed Ryder>就好像我……我不知道。感觉氧气似乎都不一样了。你明白我的意思吗？空气好像变得稀薄了。

00:50:55.550 --> 00:51:23.900
<v Ed Ryder>I'll tell you what's been a real kick for me, getting up, cooking breakfast. Making pancakes, and eggs, and bacon, and stuff like that. It sounds drab maybe to the common-- everybody would think of that as, "That's really drab." But for me, that's really exciting. And that's one of the things I always dreamed of doing. I also dreamt of finding a very lovely lady, and I have. Inside, I'm happy. Inside.
<v Ed Ryder>我告诉你什么对我来说是一种真正的乐趣，那就是起床，做早餐。做煎饼、鸡蛋、培根之类的东西。对普通人来说，这听起来可能很枯燥——大家都觉得，“那真是太枯燥了。”但对我来说，这非常令人兴奋。这是我一直梦想做的事情之一。我还梦想着找到一位可爱的女士，我也找到了。内心深处，我很幸福。内心深处。

00:51:23.900 --> 00:52:05.080
<v Ira Glass>This is Ed Ryder. He was in prison for murder and was doing time in Graterford Prison in Pennsylvania. And a few years ago, a key witness who had testified against him admitted to lying while under oath, and other evidence came forward. He was made a free man. Back when he was in Graterford Prison, he played trumpet, and he sang with a jazz band. And that whole time, he dreamed of this new life, a new life that he would have outside as a professional musician. And that's what he's working on. That's what he's working on right now. And he's playing. He doesn't have a CD yet, but he's playing gigs in the real world.
<v Ira Glass>这是埃德·莱德（Ed Ryder）。他因谋杀罪入狱，在宾夕法尼亚州的格雷特福德（Graterford）监狱服刑。几年前，一名曾对他作证的关键证人承认在宣誓后撒谎，其他证据也浮出水面。他恢复了自由。在格雷特福德监狱时，他吹小号，并在爵士乐队里唱歌。那整段时间里，他梦想着这种新生活，一种他在狱外作为职业音乐家拥有的新生活。这就是他正在努力的目标。他现在正在为此而努力。他还在演奏。他还没有CD，但他正在现实世界里参加演出。

00:52:05.080 --> 00:52:30.710
<v Ed Ryder>The first time I played, when I got out of prison, I didn't feel compelled to be so exact like I did when I was at Graterford. Inmates are the worst, critical people in the world. They criticize anything you do. In prison, you have a lot of musicians. After all, musicians, they don't make no money, so I guess the first place you find them at is in prison. But you have a lot of guys who are musicians.
<v Ed Ryder>当我出狱后第一次演奏时，我并不觉得有必要像在格雷特福德时那样精准。囚犯是世界上最刻薄的评论家。他们会批评你做的任何事。在监狱里，有很多音乐家。毕竟，音乐家赚不到钱，所以我想你最先找到他们的地方就是监狱。那里有很多搞音乐的人。

00:52:30.710 --> 00:53:04.730
<v Ed Ryder>And even though they might not be actively playing anymore, they'll be quick to criticize you, whether the chord is not right, or whether you didn't flatten the ninth, whether you didn't raise this fifth, or you guys didn't play the changes right. Although it might not have took away from the structure of the music, it's just that, I guess you can say that prisoners have a tendency to think like classical musicians would. Everything has to be perfect and exact the way it's heard. And everything has to be like that. There's no going against the grain.
<v Ed Ryder>即使他们可能不再活跃地演奏了，他们也会很快地批评你，不管是和弦不对，还是你没降九音，或者你没升五音，或者你们没按乐谱弹对。虽然这可能并没有破坏音乐的结构，但我想你可以说囚犯们倾向于像古典音乐家那样思考。一切都必须完美，必须和听到的完全一样。一切都必须那样。没有商量的余地。

00:53:04.730 --> 00:53:25.180
<v Ed Ryder>And here, in the world, people are a little different. They don't concern themselves so much with that, as much as how are you entertaining us? How are you helping us to feel better about ourselves? How are you making us feel better? At Graterford, there is nothing you can do to make them feel better, except to release them.
<v Ed Ryder>而在外面，在现实世界里，人们有点不同。他们并不太关心那些，更关心的是你如何娱乐我们？你如何帮我们感觉更好？你如何让我们开心？在格雷特福德，你除了释放他们，没什么能让他们感觉更好。

00:53:25.180 --> 00:53:46.570
<v Ed Ryder>So if you can play for a Graterford audience, you don't even look for an applause. Just as long as they don't boo you, you're all right. If they can't find nothing wrong with the music, they'll tell you, "You just didn't turn right. When you had the horn up, you didn't hold it on a 45 degree angle. It didn't look right." So they'll find something.
<v Ed Ryder>所以如果你能在格雷特福德的观众面前演奏，你甚至不需要掌声。只要他们不嘘你，你就还好。如果他们实在找不出音乐有什么毛病，他们就会告诉你，“你刚才转得不对。你举起号的时候，没把它保持在45度角。看起来不对劲。”所以他们总能找到些毛病。

00:53:46.570 --> 00:54:06.330
<v Ira Glass>Now you know how we heard people earlier in the program talk about the importance of living in the present. But when we interviewed Ed Ryder, he pointed out that, in prison, the most important thing is to keep your eye on the future, on the day that you're going to get out. And that the guys who just live in the present do really badly, because, of course, the present is so terrible.
<v Ira Glass>现在你知道我们为什么在节目早些时候听到人们谈论活在当下的重要性了。但当我们采访埃德·莱德时，他指出，在监狱里，最重要的是盯着未来，盯着你出狱的那一天。那些只活在当下的人过得很惨，因为当然了，现实是非常可怕的。

00:54:06.330 --> 00:54:36.710
<v Ed Ryder>I think when I was in prison, I dreamed more about the future in my head. I just had a lot of plans, a lot of things that I just dreamed about, I just dreamed of doing and dreamed of accomplishing. You're forced almost to. You know what I mean? You have nothing else to look forward to but tomorrow. You're constantly hoping and hoping. You're trying to plan something for the future.
<v Ed Ryder>我想我在监狱里时，脑子里更多是在梦想未来。我有很多计划，很多我梦想着做和梦想着完成的事情。你几乎被迫这样做。你明白我的意思吗？你除了明天别无他盼。你不断地希望，再希望。你努力为未来做点计划。

00:54:36.710 --> 00:54:56.610
<v Ira Glass>We asked him if he is still playing the same music now that his future is here and he's out. And we thought that maybe the songs that he used to play would just bring back these hard memories of prison that he'd rather just as soon avoid. But he said, no, he plays the same numbers now that he used to, though sometimes they mean something different to him today.
<v Ira Glass>我们问他，既然未来已经到来，他已经出狱，他是否还在演奏同样的音乐。我们原以为他过去演奏的歌曲会带回那些他宁愿避开的痛苦回忆。但他说，不，他现在还在演奏同样的曲目，尽管有时它们在今天对他有了不同的意义。

00:54:56.610 --> 00:55:23.310
<v Ed Ryder>When I was in Graterford, I always listened to it. I had a tape of "God Bless the Child That's Got His Own" by Billie Holiday. And what it meant to me when I was at Graterford-- and it has the same meaning now, but it's different. In the sense that, when I was at Graterford, when she said, "Them that's got shall get, Them that's not shall lose. So the Bible says, and it still is news. Mama may have, Papa may have, But God bless the child that has his own."
<v Ed Ryder>在格雷特福德时，我经常听它。我有一盘比莉·荷莉黛（Billie Holiday）的《God Bless the Child That's Got His Own》的磁带。它在格雷特福德时对我的意义——它现在还有同样的意义，但又有所不同。从某种意义上说，当我在格雷特福德时，当她唱到：“富有者将得更多，贫穷者将失去。圣经如是说，这依然是新闻。妈妈可能有，爸爸可能有，但上帝保佑那些有自己独立资产的孩子。”

00:55:23.310 --> 00:55:44.030
<v Ed Ryder>Well, in my mind, at Graterford, I thought that, yes, I was the child that had his own, because I was out here on my own. I had no help at the time. There was no hope. I seen no way. I was like a child. I was like a child because I had no one I can turn to. My parents had passed. I had no one I could turn to.
<v Ed Ryder>嗯，在我的脑海里，在格雷特福德，我想，是的，我就是那个有自己资产的孩子，因为我是在这里靠自己。当时我没有任何帮助。没有希望。我看不到出路。我像个孩子。我像个孩子是因为我无人可求。我的父母已经去世了。我无人可求。

00:55:44.030 --> 00:56:15.860
<v Ed Ryder>And I felt like every time I would listen to that, I felt as though she was talking directly to me. And for me, that meant that I was on my own in this prison situation. And I was going to have to make it the best way that I could. And I was going to have to muster all the energy that I could possibly muster. Now when I was released and I heard the same song again, it still had that meaning, but now it's a different-- I'm on my own differently because I'm not in prison anymore.
<v Ed Ryder>我觉得每当我听那首歌，我都觉得她是在直接对我说话。对我来说，这意味着我在那种监狱状况下是靠自己。我必须尽我所能做到最好。我必须鼓起我所能鼓起的所有能量。现在当我出狱后再次听到同一首歌时，它仍然有那种意义，但现在它不同了——我以一种不同的方式靠自己，因为我不在监狱里了。

00:56:15.860 --> 00:56:27.250
<v Ed Ryder>That's not the struggle no more. It's me. But I'm on my own now because I've got things that I have to do. I have responsibilities now that I have to take care of. I have bills that I have to pay. I have job responsibility.
<v Ed Ryder>那不再是斗争了。那是我。但我现在是靠自己，因为我有很多事情要做。我现在有必须要承担的责任。我有账单要付。我有工作职责。

00:56:27.250 --> 00:56:50.520
<v Ed Ryder>I have a lot of other things that I have to do, and I have to do these things on my own now. I don't have no prison guards waking me up in the morning, telling me, "Hey, it's time to get up," or "It's time to go eat, " or "It's time where you guys can go to a shower." I don't have that anymore. I'm on my own. So all this now is dependent upon me and my own initiative.
<v Ed Ryder>我还有很多其他要做的事情，而且我现在必须靠自己去做这些事。没有监狱看守早上把我叫醒，告诉我，“嘿，该起床了，”或者“该去吃饭了，”或者“你们可以去洗澡了。”我不再有这些了。我是靠自己。所以这一切现在都取决于我和我自己的主动性。

00:56:50.520 --> 00:59:09.610
<v Ed Ryder>[SINGING] Them that's got shall get, them that's not will lose. So the Bible said, and it still is news. Mama may have, Papa may have, but God bless the child that's got his own, that's got his own. You know the strong get more, while the weak ones fade. Empty pockets don't, they don't make the grade. Mama may have, Papa may have, but God bless the child that's got his own, that's got his own. Money, you've got lots of friends. They all keep hanging around your door. But when money's gone and all of the spendings end, they don't come around, they won't come around no more. Rich relations give a crust of bread and such. Go on and help yourself, but you better not take too much. Mama may have, Papa may have, but God bless the child that's got his own. God bless the child that's got his own.
<v Ed Ryder>[唱歌] 富有者将得更多，贫穷者将失去。圣经如是说，这依然是新闻。妈妈可能有，爸爸可能有，但上帝保佑那些有自己资产的孩子，有自己资产的孩子。你知道强者得到的更多，而弱者却消逝。空空的口袋，他们，他们无法达到标准。妈妈可能有，爸爸可能有，但上帝保佑那些有自己资产的孩子，有自己资产的孩子。钱，你有很多朋友。他们都一直徘徊在你的门前。但当钱花光了，花费结束了，他们就不会再来了，他们再也不会来了。富有的亲戚会给一点面包之类的东西。去帮你自己吧，但你最好别拿太多。妈妈可能有，爸爸可能有，但上帝保佑那些有自己资产的孩子。上帝保佑那些有自己资产的孩子。

00:59:09.610 --> 00:59:11.970
<v Ed Ryder>That's basically some of it.
<v Ed Ryder>基本上就是这样。

00:59:11.970 --> 00:59:32.200
<v Ira Glass>Ed Ryder joining us in the playhouse from the studios of WHYY in Philadelphia, speaking with me and associate producer Nancy Updike. Ed Ryder's a big Billie Holiday fanatic. That was the word he used, "fanatic," when we asked him about it. So when we asked him to play a number with his horn, with his trumpet, he played "Lover Man."
<v Ira Glass>埃德·莱德从费城WHYY工作室加入我们的剧场，与我和副制作人南希·厄普代克（Nancy Updike）交谈。埃德·莱德是比莉·荷莉黛的超级粉丝。这是我们问他时他用的词，“狂热者”。所以当我们请他用他的号，用他的小号吹奏一首曲子时，他选择了《Lover Man》。

